WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.301 --> 00:00:02.009
SPEAKER 1: Kubeflow
pipelines are made up

00:00:02.009 --> 00:00:04.948
of a series of components
that give you an ML workflow.

00:00:04.948 --> 00:00:06.990
Naturally, you'll want to
improve those workflows

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:09.240
over time, like their
performance, efficiency,

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:10.520
and accuracy.

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:13.110
Kubeflow metadata is a central
thing to help you do just that.

00:00:17.933 --> 00:00:20.100
If you want to check out
the documentation directly,

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:21.642
check out the link
below to read more

00:00:21.642 --> 00:00:23.730
about metadata on Kubeflow.

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:25.440
The goal of the
metadata project is

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.775
to help Kubeflow users
understand and manage

00:00:27.775 --> 00:00:30.150
their machine learning workflows
by tracking and managing

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:32.400
the metadata that the
workflows produce.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:34.630
Not only is it useful
to improve your results,

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:36.810
but it's important for
audit and compliance reasons

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:38.820
so you can know which
models are in production

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:40.540
and what their
characteristics are.

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:43.770
Here, metadata means
information about executions,

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:46.860
or runs, models, data
sets, and other artifacts.

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:48.600
Artifacts are the
files and objects

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.470
that form the inputs and outputs
of the components in your ML

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:52.510
workflow.

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:54.300
Kubeflow comes with
a metadata component

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:57.360
installed by default, which is
used for storing and serving

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:58.110
metadata.

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:00.930
You can write to the metadata
server from your own code.

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:03.000
And in addition, some
Kubeflow components

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.360
log metadata about their
activities automatically.

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:10.140
Then the Kubeflow UI lets
you view the logged metadata.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:12.570
In particular, Kubeflow
pipelines automatically

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:15.690
log information about a run,
including workflow artifacts,

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:17.370
executions, and lineage.

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:19.380
You can see this pipelines
feature in action

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:22.440
with the Cloud AI platform's
Pipelines Installation.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:25.860
Models and data sets can then be
visualized in the metadata web

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:28.590
UI so that you can start
tracking them out of the box

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:30.228
with no extra work required.

00:01:30.228 --> 00:01:31.770
You just need to
head to the Artifact

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:33.480
Store in the left-hand panel.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:36.030
Behind the scenes, Kubeflow
is handling the metadata

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:39.060
through ML MD, a library
for recording and retrieving

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:39.880
metadata.

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:42.720
ML MD is an integral part
of TensorFlow Extended.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:44.970
But it's designed to
be used independently.

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:47.730
Kubeflow helps you use metadata
for all components of your ML

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:48.870
workflow.

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:51.760
It comes with a metadata API
and corresponding clients.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:53.690
And you install
the metadata SDK,

00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:56.280
a Python library that makes it
easy to write to the metadata

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:57.050
server.

00:01:57.050 --> 00:01:59.490
Kubeflow Pipelines
automatically logs metadata

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:00.720
during a pipeline run.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:02.760
This lets you track the
definition of artifacts

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:04.770
for things like
pipeline versions

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:07.290
and when they were last
updated, the metadata

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:09.660
to evaluate an execution
of your pipeline,

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:12.300
like the run ID
inputs and outputs,

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:14.430
and the metadata about the
pipeline and associated

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:16.260
lineage information,
so you can clearly

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:18.420
see how artifacts and
their associated metadata

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:19.930
relate to one another.

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:22.500
In addition to Kubeflow
Pipelines automatic tracking,

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:24.420
you can manually write
to the metadata server

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:27.470
to collect additional metadata
like the metadata for a data

00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:30.120
set that forms inputs and
outputs, like a description,

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:33.810
path, and query; metrics
used to evaluate an ML

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:36.330
model, like the accuracy
and learning rate;

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:39.240
and the metadata for an ML model
that your workflow produces,

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:42.160
like the model type, hyper
parameters and labels.

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:45.178
Let's walk through an example of
writing to the metadata server.

00:02:45.178 --> 00:02:47.220
I'm running a sample
[INAUDIBLE] classifier model

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:49.080
in my Jupyter Notebook server.

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:52.200
After I execute a run, I
can log a data set, model,

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:55.620
evaluation of the model, and
metadata for serving the model.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:57.090
Once I'm in the
Artifact Store, I

00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:59.490
can see a list of items
for all the metadata events

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:00.900
that my workflows have logged.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:03.210
I can see an example
of the model, metrics,

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:07.070
and data set metadata with
the name mytable-dump.

00:03:07.070 --> 00:03:09.670
Besides using the SDK to
log metadata directly,

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:11.420
you can also add your
own metadata watcher

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:13.790
to watch Kubernetes
resource changes.

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:16.820
It extracts metadata from
some Kubernetes API objects

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:19.790
and logs them to the
Kubeflow metadata service.

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:21.800
To learn more and
try out this notebook

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:24.350
and explore its metadata,
check out the links below.

00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:26.150
And stay tuned to learn more.

